---
// Taylor's principle: the GO gauge checks the full form (the envelope), the NOGO gauge checks one local size.
const ink = 'var(--rd-ink)', muted = 'var(--rd-muted)', steel = 'var(--rd-steel)', bad = 'var(--rd-accent)', blue = 'var(--rd-blue)';
---

<figure class="viz taylor">
  <p class="viz-title">Taylor's principle</p>
  <svg viewBox="0 0 360 250" role="img" aria-labelledby="taylor-desc" style="max-width: 520px; margin: 0 auto;">
    <desc id="taylor-desc">
      Left: a bowed shaft and a full-length GO ring gauge. Each local diameter of the shaft is in tolerance, but the bow
      makes the shaft larger than its envelope, and the shaft hits the ring wall. The GO ring rejects it. Right: an oval
      hole. A round NOGO pin does not enter, because the small diameter of the oval is under the NOGO size, so a round pin
      passes the hole. A two-point NOGO gauge across the large diameter enters and rejects the hole.
    </desc>
    <text x="88" y="20" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill={ink}>GO: full form</text>
    <rect x="34" y="52" width="108" height="12" fill={steel} fill-opacity="0.6"></rect>
    <rect x="34" y="124" width="108" height="12" fill={steel} fill-opacity="0.6"></rect>
    <path d="M14,74 Q88,46 162,74 L162,118 Q88,90 14,118 Z" fill={blue} fill-opacity="0.35" stroke={blue} stroke-width="1.5"></path>
    <path d="M62,64 Q88,56 114,64 Z" fill={bad} stroke={bad} stroke-width="2"></path>
    <text x="88" y="158" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill={bad}>bow hits the ring</text>

    <text x="268" y="20" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill={ink}>NOGO: one size</text>
    <ellipse cx="268" cy="94" rx="62" ry="40" fill="none" stroke={ink} stroke-width="2"></ellipse>
    <circle cx="268" cy="94" r="46" fill="none" stroke={bad} stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5 3"></circle>
    <rect x="222" y="89" width="92" height="10" rx="2" fill={blue} fill-opacity="0.75"></rect>
    <text x="268" y="158" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill={blue}>two-point gauge enters</text>

    <text x="6" y="190" font-size="13" fill={muted}>Left: every local size is in tolerance, but</text>
    <text x="6" y="208" font-size="13" fill={muted}>the bent shaft does not fit its mating part.</text>
    <text x="6" y="230" font-size="13" fill={muted}>Right: a round NOGO pin (dashed) does not</text>
    <text x="6" y="248" font-size="13" fill={muted}>enter, so it passes the oval, oversize hole.</text>
  </svg>
  <figcaption>
    The GO gauge represents the mating part, so it checks the full form over the full length. The NOGO gauge checks one
    local size at a time, in each direction. ISO 8015 and ASME Y14.5 differ on whether the envelope applies by default,
    so the drawing tells which one applies (see <a href="../gdt/">GD&amp;T</a>).
  </figcaption>
</figure>
